🐛 LibTorch custom reshape submodule fails
Hi,
I have a simple custom reshape/view submodule that fails for a simple input in LibTorch.
My nn::Sequential model is as follows:
torch::nn::Sequential(
  (0): Permute([-1, 4, 1])
  (1): torch::nn::Conv1d(2, 3, kernel_size=2, stride=1)
  (2): Permute([-1, 4, 1])
  (3): Permute([-1, 4, 1])
  (4): torch::nn::Conv1d(3, 4, kernel_size=2, stride=1)
  (5): Permute([-1, 4, 1])
  (6): Reshape([-1, 4])
  (7): torch::nn::Linear(in_features=4, out_features=3, bias=true)
)
The implementations of Permute and Reshape modules are really simple. Following are the declarations in the header file:
struct PermuteImpl : nn::Module {
 private:
  const c10::IntArrayRef permutation_;

 public:
  explicit PermuteImpl(const c10::IntArrayRef& permutation);

  void pretty_print(std::ostream& stream) const override;

  Tensor forward(Tensor x);
};
TORCH_MODULE(Permute);

struct ReshapeImpl : nn::Module {
 private:
  const c10::IntArrayRef sizes_;

 public:
  explicit ReshapeImpl(const c10::IntArrayRef& sizes);

  void pretty_print(std::ostream& stream) const override;

  Tensor forward(Tensor x);
};
TORCH_MODULE(Reshape);
Following is the implementation in cpp file:
PermuteImpl::PermuteImpl(const c10::IntArrayRef& permutation)
    : permutation_(permutation) {}

Tensor PermuteImpl::forward(Tensor x) {
  x = x.permute(permutation_);
  return x;
}
void PermuteImpl::pretty_print(std::ostream& stream) const {
  stream << "Permute(" << permutation_ << ")";
}

ReshapeImpl::ReshapeImpl(const IntArrayRef& sizes) : sizes_(sizes) {}

Tensor ReshapeImpl::forward(Tensor x) {
  x = x.reshape(sizes_);
  return x;
}

void ReshapeImpl::pretty_print(std::ostream& stream) const {
  stream << "Reshape(" << sizes_ << ")";
}
I know about the spurious permute modules, please run with the example for now.
I get the following error. The problem seems to be with the reshape submodule. If I exclude it (and subsequent linear submodule), the program runs fines and gives the expected output (so, no problem with permute submodule). What I am perplexed with is, the definitions of permute and reshape submodules are practically identical.
